\f1\fs22 \'93When they are superimposed there is the tiny extra strand of DNA. Zooming in closely on the structure of the double helix you can see the sugar phosphate skeleton is strong and flexible. The base pairs are sturdily attached, the adenine/thymine and cytosine/guanine bonds are smooth and seamless. Now, zooming in on the extra strand and it is clear it is artificial. While the bonds are just as sturdy as human DNA, the structure is not as smooth. I conducted some underground research on it with my military sources. I expended all of my favours to get those guys to divulge the DNA is from a bio-quantum processor. This particular snippet of the code is concerned data storage, specifically compressing data for storage in a suitable biological medium. Cool huh?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable> \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Suitable biological medium?\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, usually a medium filled with artificial cells, sort of like neurons.\'94 Kathy\'92s patch had switched from MDMA to complete clarity and she was presently all business.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How will it affect real cells and actual neurons of the child?\'94 Sarah could not yet bring herself to think of the foetus as being \'91a baby\'92 or \'91her baby\'92, but \'91the clone\'92 or \'91the child\'92. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We cannot know, but if it operates within the body in the manner it does in the processor, the child\'92s brain will be able to store an enormous amount of data. He could brain dump the entirety of human knowledge and perpetually have instantaneous access. He will be a walking data base. That\'92s if it operates in the same manner. I am sure that there are infinite possibilities, most of which do not have a happy ending.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I really don\'92t know what to do. I have no problem with the concept of termination, but facing the reality is something entirely different.\'94 Sarah fully supported a woman\'92s right to choose, but struggled with the ethics of ending a life merely because of inconvenience or to avoid stagnation of career prospects. If, however, the child was going to be some kind of genetic freak, termination was a very real option, perhaps the only option.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I can stick a scanning patch to your abdomen and it will transmit data, to me, as the foetus develops. If there are any anomalous developments we can intervene at the earliest possible moment.\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You would do that for me?\'94 A great weight was lifted from Sarah\'92s shoulders. She could see a way forward.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, of course.\'94 Kathy Logan was happy to assist.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just make sure it\'92s a scanning patch.\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No hallucinating for you.\'94 Kathy laughed and licked her lips, her tongue drawing in a tiny sample of Cracker\'92s dried blood into her mouth.\
